# LLVM-RT — C runtime ABI versus LLVM preamble

File: openOODA/audit/plan_llvm_rt.oot
RT lane. Read of chs_rt.h, llvm_emit_preamble.oo, llvm_link_shim.oo,
llvm_emit_list.oo, and llvm_emit_method.oo. No product edit.
No c_emit edit. No runtime C edit.

Logline: The C layouts for OoStr, OoResS, and OoSList match the LLVM
types, so the runtime C file does not need a change.
Setup: Target is x86_64 System V on Linux. ABI means layout and call
rules between LLVM IR and the C runtime. The preamble is the IR header
that names types and symbols. The shim is a small IR file that maps
Ooda names to C names. sret is a hidden pointer for a return larger
than 16 bytes. byval is a hidden pointer for an argument larger than
16 bytes.
Beats:
  1. State the verdict.
  2. Compare the three C types to the LLVM types.
  3. Compare each live call shape to the C prototype.
  4. Name emit and shim gaps that LLVM can fix.
  5. Name residual types. Do not change C for them.

## 1. Verdict

no runtime change required

C layouts match the LLVM types.
C prototypes match System V for those layouts.
LLVM emit can fix every name and call-shape gap.

Do not edit `runtime/chs_rt.h`.
Do not edit `runtime/chs_rt*.c`.
Do not add `oo_process_exit` or `oo_str_to_int` to C.
Those two names are static inline in C emit only.

Status: done for the runtime seal. Emit and shim work stay open.

## 2. Method

Proof is static on this worktree.
Target triple in the preamble is `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`.
`int` is 32 bits. `long long` is 64 bits. A pointer is 64 bits.
No `#pragma pack` sits on OoStr, OoResS, or OoSList.
One packed struct exists in landlock code. It is not these types.

System V rule used here:
  A value of 16 bytes with integer fields uses two registers.
  A value larger than 16 bytes uses MEMORY: sret out, byval in.

## 3. Layout

| C type | C fields | Size | LLVM type | Match |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | :--- | :--- |
| OoStrHeader | uint32, uint32 | 8 | { i32, i32 } | yes |
| OoListHeader | uint32, uint32 | 8 | { i32, i32 } | yes |
| OoStr | char*, long long | 16 | { i8*, i64 } | yes |
| OoIList | i64*, i64, i64 | 24 | { i64*, i64, i64 } | yes |
| OoSList | OoStr*, i64, i64 | 24 | { %OoStr*, i64, i64 } | yes |
| OoResS | int, OoStr | 24 | { i32, %OoStr } | yes |
| OoResV | int, OoStr | 24 | { i32, %OoStr } | yes |

OoResS offsets: ok at 0, pad 4, val.data at 8, val.len at 16.
LLVM `{ i32, %OoStr }` uses the same pad because %OoStr needs
align 8.

The header structs live before heap payload. They are not fields of
the OoStr or OoSList values that cross a call.

On a failed `oo_read_file`, C stores the error text in `val`.
`ok` is 0. LLVM `extractvalue` field 1 therefore reads that text.
That matches `llvm_emit_match_expr.oo` and `.unwrap`.

## 4. Call shapes that match C

C takes every aggregate by value. LLVM must encode the System V class.

16-byte OoStr (two registers). Preamble and emit agree with C:

  void oo_str_retain(OoStr)
  void oo_str_release(OoStr)
  OoStr oo_str_lit(const char*)
  OoStr oo_str_concat(OoStr, OoStr)
  i64 oo_chars_len(OoStr)
  OoStr oo_char_at(OoStr, i64)
  OoStr oo_str_slice(OoStr, i64, i64)
  i32 oo_str_eq(OoStr, OoStr)
  i32 oo_str_contains(OoStr, OoStr)
  void oo_print_str(OoStr)
  OoStr oo_int_to_str(i64)

24-byte list (MEMORY). Preamble and `llvm_emit_list.oo` agree with C:

  void oo_ilist_new(%OoIList* sret)
  void oo_ilist_push(%OoIList* sret, %OoIList* byval, i64)
  i64 oo_ilist_get(%OoIList* byval, i64)
  i64 oo_ilist_len(%OoIList* byval)
  void oo_slist_new(%OoSList* sret)
  void oo_slist_push(%OoSList* sret, %OoSList* byval, %OoStr)
  %OoStr oo_slist_get(%OoSList* byval, i64)
  i64 oo_slist_len(%OoSList* byval)
  void oo_ilist_retain / release (%OoIList* byval)
  void oo_slist_retain / release (%OoSList* byval)

`llvm_emit_arc.oo`, `llvm_emit_let.oo`, `llvm_emit_fn.oo`, and
`llvm_emit_mainargs.oo` use the same sret and byval forms.

24-byte Result (MEMORY). The shim agrees with C:

  C: OoResS oo_read_file(long long, OoStr)
  IR to C: void @oo_read_file(%OoResS* sret, i64, %OoStr)
  C: OoResV oo_write_file(long long, OoStr, OoStr)
  IR to C: void @oo_write_file(%OoResV* sret, i64, %OoStr, %OoStr)
  C: int oo_path_exists(long long, OoStr)
  IR: i32 @oo_path_exists(i64, %OoStr)
  C: OoResS oo_sys_exec(long long, int, OoStr*)
  IR: void @oo_sys_exec(%OoResS* sret, i64, i32, %OoStr*)

The shim copies OoResV field 1 into OoResS field 1.
The layouts are the same. This is a name map, not a C change.

Scalars match: i64 with long long, i32 with int, double with double.

## 5. Gaps LLVM emit can fix

These are not C ABI breaks.

1. `.len()` on a list.
   Preamble: `i64 @oo_slist_len(%OoSList* byval(%OoSList))`.
   `llvm_emit_method.oo` emits `call i64 @oo_slist_len(%OoSList)`.
   Same fault for `@oo_ilist_len`.
   A 24-byte value in registers is not the C rule.
   DE2 owns that file. Use the byval slot that list emit already uses.

2. `oo_process_exit` and `oo_str_to_int`.
   The preamble declares both as extern C.
   `chs_rt.h` has neither.
   C emit prints them as `static inline` in the C preamble.
   The shim defines `@process_exit`, not `@oo_process_exit`.
   Add a shim or an IR body. Do not add a C symbol.

3. Ooda name versus C name.
   C emit maps `read_file` to `oo_read_file`.
   LLVM fntab keeps `@read_file` and relies on the shim.
   `emit-mods` writes `_shim.ll`.
   The one-file `emit-llvm` plus `chs_rt.c` link does not add the shim.
   Emit can call `@oo_*` with sret, or it can always link the shim.

4. `file_size` in `llvm_fntab.oo`.
   C: `long long oo_file_size(long long cap, OoStr path)`.
   Fntab: `oo_file_size=i64#%OoStr` (no cap).
   The source always passes the cap. Fix the declare. Do not change C.

5. `process_exec` has a fntab row and no C symbol.
   C has `oo_sys_exec1(long long, OoStr)`.
   Add a shim. Do not rename the C function.

6. byval and sret have no `align 8`.
   The type align is 8, so the backend should use 8.
   Emit may add `align 8` to make the intent clear.

## 6. Residual types. Do not change C

LLVM maps every Result to `%OoResS` ({ i32, %OoStr }, 24 bytes).

C also has larger Results. Leave them in C:

  OoResI: int, i64, OoStr. Size 32.
  OoResF: int, double, OoStr. Size 32.
  OoResLI / OoResLS / OoResLF: list plus OoStr. Size 48.

If LLVM later calls a C function that returns OoResI, emit must use a
32-byte type or a shim. Do not shrink the C struct.

`%OoFList` is absent. C `OoFList` stays. Same for nested list types.

Preamble grants only fs, sys, env, and net.
C already has the other grant functions. Emit may declare them.

## 7. What not to do

Do not pack OoStr, OoResS, or OoSList.
Do not change `int ok` to i8 or i64.
Do not split OoResS into ok plus two strings.
Do not replace by-value C arguments with pointers in `chs_rt.h`.
Do not edit `c_emit*.oo`.
Do not edit `llvm_emit_match_expr.oo`.
Do not overwrite `oodac/oodac`.

## 8. Next

DE2: fix `.len()` to byval. Fix the `file_size` declare.
SEC: read the shim. The shim hard-codes `sys_exec` argc 3.
MEM: list retain and release already use byval on the slot.

Runtime seal: no runtime change required.
